WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.620
- [Narrator] Over 150 moons orbit

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.000
the solar system's planets.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.713
And one of those moons calls Earth home.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.050
The moon was formed about
4.5 billion years ago when,

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.840
according to one theory, the Earth slammed

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.553
into another early planet.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.830
Debris from this collision
began to orbit Earth,

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.573
and accumulated, forming today's moon.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.240
The moon is the fifth largest natural

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.370
satellite in the solar system,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.603
with a diameter just
under the width of China.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.910
It's composed of an iron rich core,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.400
plus a mantle and crust
containing minerals

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.403
made of magnesium, oxygen and silicon.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.290
The moon's surface was
once geologically active

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.813
and covered in an ocean of magma.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.420
But today, apart from traces of water ice,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.023
the surface is completely
covered in dust and rocky debris.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.050
Countless craters dot the moon's surface.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.180
Each formed by objects such as meteroids,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.170
comets and asteroids
crashing onto the moon.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.740
The largest crater, the
South Pole Aitkin basin,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.420
spans across a quarter
of the moon's surface,

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.083
and is nearly deep enough
to fit Mount Everest inside.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.190
The moon orbits our planet at an average

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.020
distance of 30 Earths.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.340
It rotates at the same
rate that it revolves.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.820
So as it revolves around our planet,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.023
the same side of the moon
faces the Earth at all times.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.650
From the Earth's surface, we can observe

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.020
eight distinct traditionally recognized

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.640
stages of the moon's
illumination, called lunar phases.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.170
They have been observed
for thousands of years,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.713
and even provided the basis
for the earliest calendars.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.680
For most of human history,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.373
the moon could only be studied from afar.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.820
But on July 20th, 1969,
humans were able to close

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.530
that distance with the American

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.923
spaceflight mission Apollo 11.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.940
It placed humans on the moon
for the very first time.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.180
Bringing our understanding of Earth's

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.423
only natural satellite one step closer.

